## 🏁 Final Verdict

```
╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║                    OVERALL GRADE: B−                        ║
║                    SCORE: 72/100                            ║
╠══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╣
║  STRENGTHS:                                                 ║
║  ✅ Clean error hierarchy (AppError subclasses)             ║
║  ✅ asyncHandler eliminates try-catch boilerplate           ║
║  ✅ Smart performance wins (raw SQL, Promise.all, caching)  ║
║  ✅ Proper payment security (HMAC-SHA256 verification)      ║
║  ✅ Good separation: controllers → services → helpers       ║
║  ✅ Transaction usage for atomic operations                 ║
║  ✅ Idempotency checks in payment flows                     ║
║                                                             ║
║  WEAKNESSES:                                                ║
║  ❌ Zero automated tests — critical for payment system      ║
║  ❌ God Services (1000+ line files with 10+ responsibilities)║
║  ❌ Massive DRY violations (event lookup × 20, permission ×6)║
║  ❌ No dependency injection — untestable service layer      ║
║  ❌ Regex-based HTML sanitization — security vulnerability   ║
║  ❌ Registration ID race condition under concurrency         ║
║  ❌ 624-line monolithic route file                           ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
```

**Bottom line:** The code works and handles complex business logic (team events, payments, coupons) competently. The architecture shows growth from a thoughtful developer. But the technical debt — especially the lack of tests and file size bloat — will become **increasingly painful** as the system scales. **Prioritize: tests → split God services → fix the HTML sanitizer.**
